contentSections.body:
### One error envelope, everywhere

```json
{
  "error": {
    "code": "MISSING_FILE",
    "message": "The `file` field is required.",
    "details": {}
  }
}
```

Every endpoint uses this shape, including a catch-all Express error handler at the very end of the middleware chain that wraps *any* unhandled exception in the same envelope before it ever reaches a client:

```js
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  res.status(err.status || 500).json({
    error: {
      code: err.code || 'INTERNAL_ERROR',
      message: err.publicMessage || 'Something went wrong on our end.',
      details: err.details || {}
    }
  });
});
```

One shape, one place it's produced, one place an integrator needs to look. This is the same principle as the resource-naming lesson — consistency is what turns a pile of individually-reasonable endpoints into something a caller can build one piece of code against.

```js
import Stripe from 'stripe';
const stripe = new Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);

app.post('/api/checkout', authenticate, async (req, res) => {
  const session = await stripe.checkout.sessions.create({
    mode: 'subscription',
    line_items: [{ price: process.env.STRIPE_PRO_PRICE_ID, quantity: 1 }],
    success_url: `${process.env.APP_URL}/account?checkout=success`,
    cancel_url: `${process.env.APP_URL}/pricing`,
    metadata: { invoicelensUserId: req.user.id, plan: 'pro' }
  });
  res.json({ url: session.url });
});
```

The `metadata` field is doing real work here: it's how invoicelens will later know *which account* to upgrade when the payment confirms — without it, all Stripe knows is that a payment happened, with no link back to invoicelens's own user table.

A webhook endpoint is a public URL — anyone who finds it can POST whatever JSON they want to it. What makes a webhook event trustworthy isn't that it *arrived* at your endpoint, it's a cryptographic signature Stripe attaches to every real event, computed from a secret only your server and Stripe know:

```js
// Needs the raw request body — verification fails if Express's
// JSON body-parser has already parsed and re-serialized it
app.post('/api/webhooks/stripe',
  express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }),
  async (req, res) => {
    const sig = req.headers['stripe-signature'];
    let event;
    try {
      event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(
        req.body, sig, process.env.STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET
      );
    } catch (err) {
      // Signature didn't match — this request did not come from Stripe
      return res.status(400).send(`Webhook signature verification failed: ${err.message}`);
    }

    if (event.type === 'checkout.session.completed') {
      const session = event.data.object;
      const { invoicelensUserId, plan } = session.metadata;
      await db.user.update({
        where: { id: invoicelensUserId },
        data: { plan, stripeCustomerId: session.customer }
      });
    }

    res.json({ received: true });
  }
);
```

`constructEvent` throws unless the signature matches — a forged POST from anyone who found the URL fails verification immediately and never reaches the upgrade logic. This is the actual security boundary invoicelens was missing: not "is this a webhook," but "is this webhook provably from Stripe."

