```js
if (event.type === 'invoice.payment_failed') {
  const invoice = event.data.object;
  const user = await db.user.findFirst({ where: { stripeCustomerId: invoice.customer } });
  await sendEmail(user.email, 'payment-failed', {
    reason: invoice.last_finalization_error?.message ?? 'your card was declined',
    updateCardUrl: `${process.env.APP_URL}/billing`
  });
  // Access stays active — Stripe will retry automatically over the next ~2 weeks
}

if (event.type === 'customer.subscription.deleted') {
  // Fires only after Stripe's retries are genuinely exhausted
  const sub = event.data.object;
  const user = await db.user.findFirst({ where: { stripeCustomerId: sub.customer } });
  await db.user.update({ where: { id: user.id }, data: { plan: 'free' } });
  await sendEmail(user.email, 'subscription-ended', { resubscribeUrl: `${process.env.APP_URL}/pricing` });
}
```

The two events answer two genuinely different questions — "a charge just failed" versus "the subscription is actually over" — and treating them as the same event, or ignoring the first one entirely, is what turns a routine expired card into a customer who feels cut off with no explanation.

The concrete fix for invoicelens: configure the error-tracking tool (and any general request logging) to redact known-sensitive header names — `authorization`, `stripe-signature`, `cookie` — before anything is persisted, rather than relying on every future bug to happen to avoid triggering a leak:

```js
Sentry.init({
  beforeSend(event) {
    if (event.request?.headers) {
      delete event.request.headers['authorization'];
      delete event.request.headers['stripe-signature'];
      delete event.request.headers['cookie'];
    }
    return event;
  }
});
```

The general rule: redaction has to happen at the logging layer itself, applied to every request by default, not left to whichever code path happens to remember not to log something sensitive.

